# Architecture Principles (Simple)

Keep the architecture straightforward and maintainable with clear boundaries and minimal layers.

All **business logic** (Sudoku rules, eligibility, autosave policy) lives outside UI components.

## Layers and Responsibilities

- UI: screens/components and accessibility; no persistence or network calls in render paths
- Domain/Engine: Sudoku generation/solver/difficulty rating; pure TypeScript; no UI dependencies
- Application services: orchestration (start run, apply move, compute eligibility, autosave policy)
- Storage: SQLite + key/value preferences; serialization; no UI dependencies
- Sync/Backend: Supabase auth/sync/leaderboard submission validation (Edge Functions)

## Dependency Direction

- UI → application services → domain/engine + storage + sync
- Depend on interfaces/modules for external providers (Supabase client, clock/timer, storage adapters).
- Avoid circular dependencies; prefer explicit dependency injection or parameter passing.

## Simplicity & Refactoring

- Start simple; avoid premature abstraction
- Refactor when real needs emerge (reuse, complexity, testing)

## When to Create a New Layer or Extract Logic

- Extract to an application service when screen logic grows or is reused.
- Extract to a storage adapter when persistence concerns grow or need testing in isolation.
- Extract to a pure helper when logic is reusable and can be unit tested.

## Code Organization

- Group by domain/feature where practical (e.g., `engine`, `storage`, `sync`, `ui`)
- Co-locate DTOs, services, and tests near the domain they belong to
- Keep files small and focused; prefer many small units over large god-classes

## Testing

- Unit test domain/engine and eligibility rules (pure, deterministic)
- Unit test application services by mocking storage/sync providers
- Contract test Edge Functions request/response shapes and validation logic

Keep it simple: thin UI, cohesive services, focused storage/sync boundaries.

